Notes on Error vs Defect vs Bug
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. ERROR

An Error is a mistake made by a human (developer, designer, analyst, etc.) during requirement analysis, design, or coding.
or 
An error is a human mistake made during software development. It occurs when a developer, tester, or analyst makes an incorrect decision or misunderstanding.
Caused by human mistakes such as logic errors, syntax errors, or misunderstandings.
Occurs during coding, design, or requirement phase
Example: Writing wrong logic in code

Human makes a mistake = Error
Who creates it? 
Developer/Designer/Business Analyst/

SDLC Stage: Requirements: Design Coding

Examples of Error
Example 1 : in JavaScript
total = price - tax;
Developer mistakenly used - instead of +.
Correct:
JavaScript
total = price + tax;
Example 2
Requirement says:
Password must contain 8 characters.
Developer reads it as:
Password must contain 6 characters.
This misunderstanding is an Error.
Example 3
Developer writes:
JavaScript
if(age > 18)
Instead of:
JavaScript
if(age >= 18)
18-year-old users are wrongly rejected.

2. DEFECT

A Defect is a mismatch between the Expected Result and the Actual Result found during testing.
or 
A defect is a flaw in the software that occurs when expected results do not match actual results.
It is identified during testing when the application behaves incorrectly.
Tester finds a problem = Defect
Who finds it?: Tester or QA Engineer
SDLC Stage : Testing Phase

Example 1
Expected: User can login with valid username and password.
Actual: System shows "Invalid Credentials".
Result:  Defect Found

Example 2
Expected:
Clicking "Add to Cart" adds product.
Actual: Nothing happens.
Result: Defect Found

Example 3
Expected: Total price = ₹1000
Actual: Total price = ₹900
Result:  Defect Found

3. BUG
A Bug is a Defect that has been reported, logged, and accepted by the development team for fixing.
Defect reported in Jira/Bugzilla/Azure DevOps = Bug
Who creates/logs it? Tester logs it
Developer accepts it
SDLC Stage: Bug Tracking Process

Example 1
Tester finds:
Login fails with valid credentials.
Bug logged: 
Bug ID: 101
Title: Login not working with valid credentials

Example 2
Tester finds:
Search button crashes application.
Bug logged:
Bug ID: 102
Title: Search page crash

Example 3
Tester finds:
Cart total calculation is wrong.
Bug logged:
Bug ID: 103
Title: Incorrect cart calculation

Example 4 
UI button not working properly

Relationship Between Error, Defect, and Bug
Real-Life Flow =
Developer makes a mistake > Error occurs > Tester finds the issue > Defect is identified > Defect is logged in Jira > It becomes a Bug > Developer fixes it.
